WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
[Hank:] Good Morning John.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.820
I spent last weekend in Glacier National Park

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.240
A place that is dripping with scenic beauty and absolutely no cell phone signals

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.600
That might have something to do with all the extremely sharp cliffs and jutting rock formations

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.100
and a desire not to like build cell phone towers on some of the most scenic mountains in the world

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.920
There is a road. It was completed in the 1930s that bisects Glacier National Park

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.900
called the Going to the Sun Road

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.900
Its a little bit terrifying

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.420
as for a great deal of the time, especially on the way up,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.920
you're basically driving 3 feet away from a precipitous drop.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.820
If you are in the car and not driving there's this weird sensation that you need to lean away

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.020
from the cliff.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.129
Even though obviously that's not gonna
help anything if the car goes over

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:44.520
but you shouldn't lean away from the cliff. You should lean toward the cliff because

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.450
that's where the best view is. It's a
little strange that this road was even built

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.370
It continues to be an engineering
marvel because of avalanches and

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.390
freeze-thaw cycles the road needs
constant repair and the road doesn't

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.920
actually go anywhere except to the top:

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.489
Logan Pass which during the winter is
regularly covered in more than 80 feet

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:11.100
of snow. The Going to the Sun Road closes in September and doesn't open again until July

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.020
It almost seems like a
uniquely American thing.  There's this

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.610
beautiful place but people can't get to
it unless they're ridiculously dedicated.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.509
So let's spend millions of dollars
building a miracle of a road, in fact

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:30.000
let's give it a miraculous sounding name.
We have to democratize access to this

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.390
grandeur in this beauty and these cute
ground squirrels and these majestic

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.259
mountain goats. But you cannot snapchat
them while you're up there because your cell

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:40.409
phone doesn't work you have to wait
until you get back to your hotel room

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:45.119
and then when you get there you'll check
the news and find out that once again

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:48.509
Donald Trump has said something
disgusting and inexcusable that somehow

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:52.860
hasn't disqualified him from seeking the
presidency of this beautiful place.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.060
Listen, I'm not much of a nationalist.
Part of what I like about Glacier

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.200
National Park is that the ecosystem it
contains stretches between international

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.149
borders and into Canada's Waterton Lakes
National Park indeed a commitment to

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:10.410
being a proactive part of the global
systems on which we rely economically

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.560
socially and ecologically is part of
what I

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.720
love about being an American and I
really do love this country though that

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.860
gets harder during election years which
always highlight what I dislike about

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.840
being an American. We work so hard to
demonize each other that everyone comes

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.580
out looking like demons. And yes, that
looks a lot worse this time around than

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.349
it did last time, and yes, I do feel
particularly discouraged but it's easy

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:42.090
to forget what this country has done:
what we have built, what it costs us, and

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.830
how easy it is to be proud of it. All you
have to do is not look at twitter for a

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.130
few hours and instead look at what we've
built

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:54.269
Yes, we have problems, and yes, it makes
sense that we spend a lot of time

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:59.099
discussing them because after all they
are the problems but god damn it if I

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.540
don't love this country even in the face
of the popularity of a despicable man

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.900
God damn it if I'm not proud to be an American.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.400
John, I'll see you on Tuesday

